WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.987
Independent evidence, and what each kind can tell you.

2
00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:04.824
Arena-style leaderboards.

3
00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:23.899
The Arena leaderboard (formerly LMArena and Chatbot Arena) shows two anonymous models’ answers to a user’s prompt, asks the user to vote, and fits a statistical rating model (Bradley-Terry) to the accumulated pairwise votes, as described by Chiang and colleagues in 2024.

4
00:00:24.179 --> 00:00:29.000
It measures what real people prefer on their own prompts, which static benchmarks miss.

5
00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.008
Its caveats are documented.

6
00:00:31.288 --> 00:00:43.875
The Arena team’s own “style control” analysis found that answer length is the dominant style factor in votes, with markdown formatting a smaller effect, and that rankings shift once style is controlled.

7
00:00:44.155 --> 00:01:00.283
And “The Leaderboard Illusion” (Singh and colleagues, 2025) reported that some providers privately tested many variants before release and could withhold weaker scores, and that a few large providers received a disproportionate share of the battle data.

8
00:01:00.563 --> 00:01:01.885
Measurement services.

9
00:01:02.165 --> 00:01:11.531
Artificial Analysis publishes speed, latency, and price comparisons across providers’ endpoints, plus an intelligence index built from benchmarks.

10
00:01:11.811 --> 00:01:23.501
Its speed figures are measurements; its prices are the providers’ listed prices, and its blended price assumes a fixed mix of cache-hit, input, and output tokens that may not match yours.

11
00:01:23.781 --> 00:01:24.997
Task benchmarks.

12
00:01:25.277 --> 00:01:35.901
SWE-bench (Jimenez and colleagues, 2023) asks models to resolve real GitHub issues and checks the fix by running the repository’s tests.

13
00:01:36.181 --> 00:01:49.707
SWE-bench Verified is a human-filtered subset of 500 of those tasks, made with OpenAI, after reviewers found that some original tasks were underspecified or had unreliable tests.

14
00:01:49.987 --> 00:02:06.947
Benchmarks like this are concrete and reproducible, but any public test set can leak into training data: Deng and colleagues (2023) found commercial models could guess hidden answer options on MMLU far above chance, a sign the items had been seen.

15
00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:08.763
Provider-published evals.

16
00:02:09.043 --> 00:02:15.912
Launch posts and model cards report scores the vendor ran, with prompts, scaffolds, and settings the vendor chose.

17
00:02:16.192 --> 00:02:23.595
They are useful and usually honest, but they are not independent, and different vendors’ numbers are rarely run the same way.
